# Project Context

> Adapt this file to your project after the first version of `intake/problem-intake.md` is complete. Ask the agent to propose updates and have a human review them before they are committed.

This repository was created from the Project VIC Agentic AI Development Project Template. It helps a human start a new mission-tool project with an AI agent, Spec Kit, GitHub planning, synthetic test data, CASE/UCO/CAC awareness, and safety guardrails.

This file provides repository-local context. It supplements, but does not replace, the other policies in `policies/`.

## Template Status

- Do not assume an application stack until `intake/problem-intake.md`, the project constitution, and the first Spec Kit plan identify one.
- Do not initialize Spec Kit before the first version of `intake/problem-intake.md` is filled out.
- Once the project is understood, replace the template guidance below with project-specific technologies, commands, surfaces, data flows, and validation steps.
- Keep the safety, privacy, ontology, testing, no-stubs, release, cross-platform, and human-review policies active unless the project owner explicitly changes them.

Do not introduce frameworks, databases, queues, package managers, build systems, deployment services, model pipelines, or major architectural dependencies without an explicit intake, spec, or plan requirement.

## Spec Kit Generated Context

If the optional Spec Kit `agent-context` extension is installed, Spec Kit writes technology context into one agent file (for example `.cursor/rules/specify-rules.mdc`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, or `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, depending on the integration chosen at `specify init`) between `<!-- SPECKIT START -->` and `<!-- SPECKIT END -->` markers. That is expected. Generated context describes the stack; it never overrides the policies in `policies/`. Preserve Spec Kit-generated sections unless intentionally regenerating them, and keep the separate `PROJECT-VIC-POLICY` marker blocks intact.

## Repository Surfaces To Establish

Decide during intake and planning which surfaces are required:

- GUI or other human-facing interface.
- API or integration surface.
- CLI, pipeline, batch, or offline workflow.
- MCP server, prompts, tools, or resources for agent use.
- Container, deployment, setup, or operations surface.
- Reports, exports, generated artifacts, schemas, ontology output, or audit/provenance records.
- Documentation, planning, Spec Kit, GitHub issues, milestones, and release materials.

When one surface changes, check whether other planned surfaces need equivalent updates or a documented exception.

## Common Commands To Establish

Before implementation starts, define project-specific commands for:

- setup and dependency installation
- linting and formatting
- unit, integration, contract, and end-to-end tests (one canonical fast test command, per `policies/testing-program.md`)
- API, GUI, CLI, MCP, report/export, ontology, and container validation where applicable
- GitHub Actions workflows, only when the project needs them (see `.github/workflows/README.md`)
- human verification with `docs/manual-test-checklist.md` and a per-release copy of `docs/templates/manual-test-release-annex.md`
- release versioning through the `v1.0.0` MVP, per `policies/versioning-release-management.md` and `planning/release-roadmap-template.md`

Do not treat a compile-only check as a substitute for real tests when behavior changes.

## Style and Implementation Guidance

- Follow existing local patterns before adding abstractions.
- Keep business logic in shared service/application layers.
- Avoid duplicating behavior independently across GUI, API, CLI/pipeline, and MCP surfaces.
- Keep source evidence paths read-only and validate input roots when evidence handling is in scope.
- Reject path traversal and outputs outside approved case/run/output directories.
- Prefer structured parsers and serializers over ad-hoc string manipulation.
- Prefer explicit typed errors over silent failures.
- Do not add stubs, fake success responses, or TODO-only implementation paths.
- Keep docs, changelog, environment templates, API schemas, GUI types, pipeline artifacts, and release notes aligned.
- Use Tier T0/T1 data for everything in this repository. See `docs/test-data-handling.md`.

## Documentation and Release Hygiene

Update documentation when behavior changes for installation, deployment or container profiles, configuration, GUI usage, API usage, CLI/pipeline usage, hash lookup behavior, evidence input and artifact output, CASE/UCO/CAC exports, ontology validation, offline or air-gapped operation, GPU/CPU behavior, and known limitations.

User-visible or operator-visible changes should include a `CHANGELOG.md` entry.

